{"qid":"Q792","names":["Republic of El Salvador"],"title":"Republic of El Salvador","endonym":{"native":"República de El Salvador","lang":"es"},"capsule":"El Salvador, the smallest and most densely populated country in Central America, declared independence from Spain in 1821 and from the Central American Federation in 1840. The country's twentieth century was dominated by military dictatorships serving agro-export oligarchies, culminating in a brutal civil war (1979–1992) in which government forces and right-wing death squads killed an estimated 75,000 people; the Chapultepec Peace Accords of January 1992 ended the conflict. El Salvador became the first country to adopt Bitcoin as legal tender in 2021 under President Nayib Bukele, who also pursued a controversial mass-incarceration policy against gang structures.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. El Salvador's boundaries are internationally recognized and well-documented; a brief border dispute with Honduras was resolved by the ICJ in 1992.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: El Salvador","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Salvador"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Salvadoran Civil War","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salvadoran_Civil_War"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Chapultepec Peace Accords","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapultepec_Peace_Accords"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Nayib Bukele","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nayib_Bukele"}],"britannica":"place/El-Salvador","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"El Salvador","rulers":[{"qid":"Q332111","name":"Francisco Morazán","start":1839,"end":1840,"image":null},{"qid":"Q887495","name":"Francisco Malespín